==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[sadnesse]] ''(obsolete)''

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|sadnesse}}, equivalent to {{af|en|sad|-ness}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsædnəs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The state or [[emotion]] of being [[sad]].
#: {{syn|en|forlornness|melancholy|Thesaurus:sadness}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=1|scene=1|passage=But sorrow that is couch'd in seeming gladness<br>Is like that mirth fate turns to sudden '''sadness'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Athenian Sport[:] {{...}}|passage=I mean not then under the name of '''''Sadneſs''''' to defend effeminate Bewailings and Lamentations; let them that ſubject themſelves to this weakeſt Impatience, be alſo ſubject to the ''Lycian'' Law, that bound theſe kind of Lamenters to be array'd like Women: {{...}}|year=1707|author=w:John Dunton|page=423|chapter=Mourning Joy: or a Paradox in Praiſe of Sadneſs|chapterurl=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/Athenian_Sport/DzcJAAAAQAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA413}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An event in one's [[life]] that causes sadness.
#: {{syn|en|misfortune|woe|Thesaurus:woe}}
#: {{ux|en|She has experienced many '''sadnesses''' in her forty years.}}

